A persistent `{{ .Shell }}` terminal. `command` is what you type: at the prompt a command line (Enter implied); while a program runs, its input, with `<enter>` or `\n` where a person would press it and other keys in angle brackets (`<escape>`, `<tab>`, `<up>`, `<ctrl+c>`, `<f1>`, ...).

Sessions are independent terminals, one per `session` name (default `main`): cwd, environment, held credentials and running programs are per session. Keep long-lived programs - dev servers, watchers - in their own named session. A line typed while a session's command runs is queued for the next prompt; an answer to a waiting program needs an explicit `<enter>`. A call returns when the process exits (with its exit code), stops for input, takes over the screen (a rendered {{ .DefaultCols }}x{{ .DefaultRows }} frame), or goes idle; an empty call waits for the next of those. A bare `sleep` is refused: waiting on a condition belongs in the command that checks it. A session whose shell has exited reports its final output and exit code once, then reopens fresh in its original directory. `reset` kills the named session and opens a fresh one; last resort.

Pagers are replaced with `cat` and color is off, so output arrives as plain text. A result ends with `<cwd>…</cwd>` only when the shell moved. Output over {{ .MaxOutputLength }} characters keeps a head and a long tail.
